Z||000000||four50four||11-15-1999||05:45 PM||||what kind of hp and torque is available from a 3.4 camaro/firebird engine? is 250 hp and higher torque unrealistic? how much $$$ will it cost to get figures like this? any info would be great. thanks!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||WisconsinGT||11-15-1999||06:01 PM||||You can probably get 250hp+ with a turbo add on.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||11-15-1999||08:14 PM||||Realistically, a 3.4 engine built to be reliable and streetable with 9:1 compression ratio, headers, GM HO cam, pocket ported HO Fiero heads will probably be closer to 220 HP and 230 TQ at the most.<P>To get more than that you'll have to up the compression ratio, which I don't recommend for a daily drive because it won't run right on pump gas, or go with a more radical cam, which might mess with your computer. The other options are turbos, superchargers, or nitrous. I'd use forged pistons with any of those last three options and of course that starts getting very expensive.<P>Don't worry so much about horsepower for a street engine. You want torque. I love Phil's quote, "Torque gets you down the track quick, but horsepower gets the cars off the car lot quick." Or words to effect.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dan||11-16-1999||10:00 AM|||| The 3.4 camaro engine is the TDC motor (Twin Dual Cam).Stock they say is about 210 HP. The other 3.4 is about 160 HP I think.Check out this web site at <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/gpxss/history/history.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/gpxss/history/history.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||four50four||11-16-1999||03:43 PM||||93-95 (i think) was a 3.4 pushrod engine. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eddie||11-16-1999||09:10 PM||||I think if you put the 3.4 4 cam motor in a fiero and still need more power you should invest in a rocket or a jet!!!! thats alot of power in the 3.4 4 cam motor||||reg||4
Z||000006||GT Bastard||11-17-1999||01:55 AM||||Dude, there was never a TDC camaro engine... Those engines would be a b!tch to find.  People need to be more realistic about their cars!!! Batboy provided some good info, but "a turbo add on"???  c'mon people!!  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||11-17-1999||03:56 AM||||Actually, there is a 3.4 DOHC out of the Pontiac Grand Prixs and a 3.4 pushrod out of the f-bodies. There is a 3.4 air to water intercooled turbo with water injection (and a partridge in a pear tree)push rod engine nearing completion. There is also in existance a 3.4 DOHC turbo. They both scoot out like bats out of Hell. But to put a number on it? <P>Numbers don't mean anything at the stoplight.<P>You can guess all you want but the only accurate numbers come from a dyno, at the wheel. Isn't nice that we keep the Fieros evolving like this though?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dan||11-17-1999||09:29 AM|||| I stand corrected.Sorry for the bad dope.I hope the link was useful though.Keep me honest guys.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||11-17-1999||03:23 PM||||I like that "bat out of hell" phrase. He's right, only the dyno really knows what power your engine will put out. I was using a fairly accurate computer program for my "realistic estimate" but that's still just hypothetical.<P>Also, the figures I gave are gross power readings (measured at the engine flywheel). Chassis dynos give net power numbers (measured at the drive wheels) which will be a little less than the gross values.||||reg||1
